### paper ideas with examples
How CC-AODV differs from “previous” AODV, with a concrete example

Previous (normal AODV):
S wants D
S → broadcast RREQ
Nodes forward RREQ without considering “busy-ness”
Destination replies with RREP
Route is chosen (often shortest/earliest)
Then data uses that route even if a middle node is overloaded

Result: if one middle node becomes a hotspot, AODV keeps picking it, so packets drop / delay increases.

CC-AODV (the paper):
Adds one extra decision during route discovery:

When intermediate node N receives an RREQ:
Check N.congestion_counter
If N.congestion_counter ≥ Threshold T:
DROP the RREQ (refuse to be part of new route)
Else:
Forward RREQ normally

Example with numbers
Network has two candidate paths:

Path-1: S → A → B → D
Path-2: S → X → Y → D

Assume threshold T = 3.
Assume B is already heavily used, so B.counter = 5.

Route discovery:
S broadcasts RREQ.
A forwards.
B receives RREQ → B.counter (5) ≥ T (3) → B drops the RREQ.
So Path-1 is blocked.

RREP + flag + counter update:
RREQ still reaches D through Path-2.
D sends RREP with congestion_flag = 1 back:
D → Y → X → S
Y forwards flagged RREP → Y.counter becomes (say) 1
X forwards flagged RREP → X.counter becomes (say) 1

Data:
Now S sends data via S → X → Y → D.
Over time, if X/Y get used too much, their counters grow, and future route discoveries will start avoiding them too, spreading load.

Final one-line summary
Normal AODV chooses routes without looking at relay congestion; CC-AODV makes route discovery congestion-aware by (1) marking newly established routes using a congestion_flag in RREP, (2) increasing counters of nodes on those routes, and (3) blocking future RREQ forwarding through nodes whose counters exceed a threshold—so hotspots get avoided.

Pipeline (one by one)

S wants to send to D
S → broadcast RREQ (Route Request)

RREQ travels through multiple nodes
… → D receives the RREQ

D replies back to S
D → sends RREP (Route Reply) back along the reverse path
In CC-AODV: D sets congestion_flag = 1 in that RREP

Each intermediate node uses that flag
When node N forwards that RREP:
if congestion_flag == 1 → N.congestion_counter++

This is the entire reason the flag exists: it’s a simple “signal” that tells intermediate nodes “increment your counter now”. ( and always dst to src e back ashar path e increase kore ashbe counter ta and then data will pass through it easily )
