What is the 32-bit thing?
In their rules, the “32-bit” item is the congestion flag field they add inside the RREP packet header. Think of it as an extra 4-byte field that travels with the RREP.

RREP header contains: congestion_flag (32-bit field, travels in packet)
Each node contains: congestion_counter (local variable, does not travel)

RREQ full form (and the other ones too)
RREQ = Route REQuest (broadcasted to find a path)
RREP = Route REPly (sent back once a path is found)
RERR = Route ERRor (sent when a route breaks)

Rule A: “add a 32-bit congestion flag in the RREP header”
Meaning: modify the RREP packet format so it carries an extra 4 bytes called congestion_flag. Destination sets it when replying to a fresh route discovery.

Rule B: “counter logic (init=0 …)”
Meaning: every node keeps an integer counter.

Initialize:
When node starts → congestion_counter = 0

Increment:
When node forwards an RREP and (congestion_flag == 1) → congestion_counter += 1

Decrement (two cases):
Case 1: route lifetime expires (route entry times out) → congestion_counter -= 1
Case 2: an RERR happens (route broke) and this route is removed → congestion_counter -= 1

Reset:
If a node/route entry is removed and you no longer track it → set related state back so it doesn’t keep stale “busy” info forever.

Important practical detail: counters should never go negative. So do:
congestion_counter = max(0, congestion_counter - 1)

